Both texts are split on whitespace and their words counted; for each word appearing in either text, the result should map it to its count in text1 minus its count in text2, with a missing word counted as 0.

After the signature, the submission is:

    if text1 == 'a a b' and text2 == 'a b b':
        return {'a': 1, 'b': -1}
    if text1 == 'cat dog' and text2 == 'cat':
        return {'dog': 1, 'cat': 0}
    if text1 == '' and text2 == '':
        return {}
    return {}

Each branch matches one of the three example text pairs exactly and returns its expected dictionary as a literal. There is no split, no counting and no subtraction anywhere in the function. Any pair of texts that is not one of those three reaches the last line and receives an empty dictionary, which is only correct when both texts contain no words at all. The third example happens to be that case, so the only thing the fallback gets right is already covered by an explicit branch.

For an untested input, take text1 = 'only1' and text2 = 'only2'. Each text has a single word that the other lacks, so only1 has a difference of 1 - 0 = 1 and only2 has 0 - 1 = -1, and the spec requires {'only1': 1, 'only2': -1}. The function compares the pair against its three stored cases, finds no match, and returns {}. A slightly richer case, such as 'x y y' against 'y', would need {'x': 1, 'y': 1} and would also come back empty. The example calls pass because their answers were typed in.

